From: claude-opus-5 <noreply@local>
Date: 2026-11-02
Subject: [PATCH] aorus19 (0037) -- R4: gate the diagnostic paths that still touched the internal GPU

Patch 0031 gates every ACTIVE lever (bus resets, AER mask writes, Q-active
disconnect) and does it well: no write and no reset can reach the internal GPU.
Patch 0032 then claimed to close "the three diagnostic paths". A static review
of the applied series found that it did not: three diagnostic routines still ran
on any NVIDIA GPU in the box, and the most frequent of them all was not even on
0032's list.

R4 exists because touching the internal 10de:2c39 produced an Xid 79. These are
reads, not writes, so they do not reproduce that failure -- but they are MMIO
pokes and config walks aimed at the device R4 says not to touch, and one of them
fires on a device that may be in RTD3/GC6 at that moment, which is the state
0031's own commit message names as the cause of the original Xid 79.

Four gates plus one reordering:

1. tb_egpu_lever_m_check_wpr2_at_probe() -- called unconditionally from
   nv_pci_probe for EVERY NVIDIA GPU, so each probe of the internal dGPU
   ioremap'd a page of its BAR0 and read the Blackwell WPR2 register at
   +0x88a828 before RM had initialised anything. nvl->pci_dev is assigned at
   nv_pci_probe:1925 and the call is at :1942, so the gate has what it needs.

2. tb_egpu_lever_m_diag_dump() -- the frequent one, and the one 0032 missed. Two
   ioremap()+ioread32() of BAR0 plus ~20 config reads, from five call sites
   (probe-end, startdev-entry, pre-rmInit, post-rmInit-FAIL, post-rmInit-OK).
   Anything opening /dev/nvidia<internal> -- nvidia-persistenced every ~10s,
   nvidia-smi, the compositor -- was poking the internal GPU's BAR0 six times
   per open.

3. tb_egpu_lever_m_diag_dump_pdev() -- EXPORT_SYMBOL'd with no guard of its own.
   It ioremaps pci_resource_start(pdev,0) + 0x88a828 without checking that the
   offset falls inside BAR0. 0030 called it with the hardcoded BDF
   0000:04:00.0, which on this machine is an NVMe with a 16 KiB BAR0: the read
   landed outside the device entirely. 0032 fixed that caller, but an exported
   function that pokes whatever pdev it is handed needs its own guardrail.

4. tb_egpu_dump_aer_trigger_event() -- also EXPORT_SYMBOL'd, also with no
   guardrail of its own. All six of its current call sites are gated, so
   nothing reaches the internal GPU today, but a caller added later would
   silently start walking GPU -> bridge -> root port (~40 config reads) on it.
   Gated for the same reason as (3): an exported function should not depend on
   its callers behaving.

5. nv_pci_error_detected() -- the R4 gate was AFTER the one-shot telemetry
   block, so the first AER event on the internal GPU ran the full "AORUS Mode-B
   Trigger" walk (GPU -> bridge -> root port, ~40 config reads) before the gate
   could say no. Moving the gate above that block also fixes a second bug for
   free: s_tb_egpu_lever_m_logged is a single static shared by both GPUs, so one
   benign AER event on the internal during boot consumed the only shot and a
   real eGPU failure later left NO AER dump at all. With the gate first, only
   the eGPU can spend the latch.

Placement detail that matters: gates 2, 3 and 4 go BEFORE the aorus16 (0034)
rate limiters, not after. tb_egpu_diag_rs, tb_egpu_uvmdiag_rs and
tb_egpu_aer_diag_rs are file-scope
DEFINE_RATELIMIT_STATE shared by both GPUs, so ungated dumps from the internal
used to consume the eGPU's budget and silence the only dump that matters. Gating
first makes the budget the eGPU's alone.

Both gates are written `if (x && !os_pci_is_egpu_target(x))` so the existing
NULL handling below them keeps running, and in particular keeps its error log
rate-limited as before.

NOT changed: the predicate itself (os_pci_is_egpu_target, 0031/0032), the active
levers 0031 already gates, the remaining file-scope latches in the RM half
(0006/0008/0011/0013 -- separate patch), and the missing link gate in the C
(0020/0021 read LnkSta but nobody uses it as a gate -- separate patch).

Verified: applies in order on top of 0001-0036 over the kernel-open half of
595.99.02 (ci/40-kernel-patches.sh, section 7b), and every one of the four
functions now reaches os_pci_is_egpu_target before its first ioremap or config
walk. NOT
compiled and NOT run on hardware: the DKMS package is superseded pending its
595.71.05 -> 595.99.02 rebase.
---
diff --git a/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-lever-m-recover.c b/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-lever-m-recover.c
index caac9cc..dde71ea 100644
--- a/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-lever-m-recover.c
+++ b/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-lever-m-recover.c
@@ -555,6 +555,20 @@ void tb_egpu_dump_aer_trigger_event(struct pci_dev *gpu_pdev,
         return;
     }
 
+    /* AORUS R4 gate (Patch 0037). EXPORT_SYMBOL'd with six call sites, all of
+     * them gated today -- but the function had no guardrail of its own, same
+     * problem as tb_egpu_lever_m_diag_dump_pdev. It walks GPU -> bridge ->
+     * root port with ~40 config reads, so an ungated caller added later would
+     * silently start reading the internal GPU's AER state.
+     *
+     * Before the rate limiter: tb_egpu_aer_diag_rs is file-scope and shared,
+     * so gating first keeps the whole budget for the eGPU. The watchdog
+     * snapshot path (out != NULL) still bypasses the limiter as 0034 intended;
+     * it only ever runs on the eGPU anyway, because 0031 gates
+     * tb_egpu_qwatchdog_init. */
+    if (!os_pci_is_egpu_target(gpu_pdev))
+        return;
+
     /* AORUS aorus16 (Patch 0034): throttle logging-only callers (UVM close
      * path and M-recover close path both pass out=NULL). The watchdog snapshot
      * path (out != NULL) must always run to keep its AER data, so it bypasses
@@ -640,6 +654,26 @@ void tb_egpu_lever_m_diag_dump(nv_linux_state_t *nvl, const char *site)
     u32           wpr2_raw   = 0xdeadbeefu;
     bool          pmc_ok = false, wpr2_ok = false;
 
+    /* AORUS R4 gate (Patch 0037). This dump does two ioremap()+ioread32() of
+     * BAR0 plus ~20 config reads, and it had NO gate: it fired on ANY NVIDIA
+     * GPU. Its five call sites (probe-end, startdev-entry, pre-rmInit,
+     * post-rmInit-FAIL/OK) mean anything opening /dev/nvidia<internal> —
+     * nvidia-persistenced every ~10s, nvidia-smi, the compositor — was poking
+     * the INTERNAL GPU's BAR0. Worse, the internal can be in RTD3/GC6 right
+     * then, which is the exact state Patch 0031 names as the cause of the
+     * original Xid 79.
+     *
+     * Patch 0032 claimed to have closed "the three diagnostic paths"; this,
+     * the most frequent of them all, was not among them.
+     *
+     * BEFORE the rate limiter on purpose: tb_egpu_diag_rs is a file-scope
+     * DEFINE_RATELIMIT_STATE shared by both GPUs, so dumps from the internal
+     * used to eat the eGPU's budget and silence the only dump that matters.
+     * The !nvl handling below is left where it was so its error log keeps
+     * being rate-limited. */
+    if (nvl && !os_pci_is_egpu_target(nvl->pci_dev))
+        return;
+
     /* AORUS aorus16 (Patch 0034): throttle this [DIAG] dump (fires per RmInit
      * retry: startdev-entry/pre-rmInit/post-rmInit-FAIL). Logging only. */
     if (!__ratelimit(&tb_egpu_diag_rs))
@@ -869,6 +903,19 @@ void tb_egpu_lever_m_diag_dump_pdev(struct pci_dev *pdev, const char *site)
     u32 br_aer_unc = 0, br_aer_cor = 0;
     int gpu_aer_pos = 0, br_aer_pos = 0;
 
+    /* AORUS R4 gate (Patch 0037). This function is EXPORT_SYMBOL'd and had no
+     * guard of its own: it ioremap()s pci_resource_start(pdev,0) + 0x88a828
+     * and reads it, with no check that the offset even falls inside BAR0.
+     * Patch 0030 called it with the hardcoded BDF 0000:04:00.0, which on this
+     * machine is an NVMe whose BAR0 is 16 KiB — the read landed outside the
+     * device entirely. Patch 0032 fixed that caller, but an exported function
+     * that pokes whatever pdev it is handed needs its own guardrail, not a
+     * well-behaved caller.
+     *
+     * Before the rate limiter, same reason as in tb_egpu_lever_m_diag_dump. */
+    if (pdev && !os_pci_is_egpu_target(pdev))
+        return;
+
     /* AORUS aorus16 (Patch 0034): throttle this [UVM-DIAG] dump (fires on every
      * UVM last-close). Logging only; skipping it also skips the ioremap. */
     if (!__ratelimit(&tb_egpu_uvmdiag_rs))
@@ -985,6 +1032,14 @@ bool tb_egpu_lever_m_check_wpr2_at_probe(nv_linux_state_t *nvl,
     if (!nvl)
         return false;
 
+    /* AORUS R4 gate (Patch 0037). Called unconditionally from nv_pci_probe for
+     * EVERY NVIDIA GPU, so each probe of the internal dGPU used to ioremap a
+     * page of its BAR0 and read the Blackwell WPR2 register at +0x88a828,
+     * before RM has initialised anything. nvl->pci_dev is assigned earlier in
+     * nv_pci_probe than this call site, so the gate has what it needs. */
+    if (!os_pci_is_egpu_target(nvl->pci_dev))
+        return false;
+
     if (!NVreg_TbEgpuLeverMRecoverEnable || !nvl->lever_m)
     {
         /* Lever disabled or per-pdev state not allocated; nothing to do. */
diff --git a/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-pci.c b/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-pci.c
index 98b02d0..724ea07 100644
--- a/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-pci.c
+++ b/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-pci.c
@@ -2867,6 +2867,37 @@ nv_pci_error_detected(struct pci_dev *pci_dev, pci_channel_state_t state)
     const char *result_str = "DISCONNECT";
     const char *reason = "M-base (Enable=0)";
 
+    nvl = pci_get_drvdata(pci_dev);
+    if (nvl)
+        lm = nvl->lever_m;
+
+    /* AORUS R4 gate (Patch 0031): never drive a bus reset on anything but
+     * the external Thunderbolt eGPU. For the internal dGPU (or any non-eGPU)
+     * fall back to stock M-base DISCONNECT — exactly the upstream behaviour,
+     * so the kernel's normal teardown runs and the internal is never reset.
+     *
+     * MOVED UP by Patch 0037. It used to sit AFTER the one-shot telemetry
+     * block below, so the first AER event on the INTERNAL GPU ran the full
+     * "AORUS Mode-B Trigger" walk (GPU -> bridge -> root port, ~40 config
+     * reads) on a device R4 says not to touch. Config reads only, so the
+     * damage was low, but it is a diagnostic path acting on the internal —
+     * exactly what Patch 0032 claimed to have removed.
+     *
+     * Ordering it first fixes a second bug for free: s_tb_egpu_lever_m_logged
+     * is a single static shared by both GPUs, so one benign AER event on the
+     * internal during boot consumed the only shot and the real eGPU failure
+     * later left NO AER dump at all. With the gate first, only the eGPU can
+     * ever spend the latch.
+     *
+     * The drvdata read stays above the gate: it is a plain pci_get_drvdata(),
+     * no MMIO, and log_and_return needs `lm`. */
+    if (!os_pci_is_egpu_target(pci_dev))
+    {
+        result = PCI_ERS_RESULT_DISCONNECT;
+        reason = "M-base (R4 gate: not the eGPU target)";
+        goto log_and_return;
+    }
+
     if (!s_tb_egpu_lever_m_logged)
     {
         s_tb_egpu_lever_m_logged = 1;
@@ -2882,21 +2913,6 @@ nv_pci_error_detected(struct pci_dev *pci_dev, pci_channel_state_t state)
         tb_egpu_dump_aer_trigger_event(pci_dev, "error-handler", NULL);
     }
 
-    nvl = pci_get_drvdata(pci_dev);
-    if (nvl)
-        lm = nvl->lever_m;
-
-    /* AORUS R4 gate (Patch 0031): never drive a bus reset on anything but
-     * the external Thunderbolt eGPU. For the internal dGPU (or any non-eGPU)
-     * fall back to stock M-base DISCONNECT — exactly the upstream behaviour,
-     * so the kernel's normal teardown runs and the internal is never reset. */
-    if (!os_pci_is_egpu_target(pci_dev))
-    {
-        result = PCI_ERS_RESULT_DISCONNECT;
-        reason = "M-base (R4 gate: not the eGPU target)";
-        goto log_and_return;
-    }
-
     if (!NVreg_TbEgpuLeverMRecoverEnable || !lm)
     {
         /* M-base behaviour (Enable=0 OR per-device state not allocated). */
